  "notes": "Italo-dance with a light female lead — the \"Italo-dance meets Eurodance\" template. `confidence` is low because the research rates this entry medium-low and says so plainly: \"era and idiom documented; the parameters are Italo-dance convention and marked accordingly\". Everything below the era and the four-on-the-floor/clap-on-2&4 pair is therefore INFERRED. Two documented facts do the differentiating. The tonality is the outlier the entry flags — \"bright major-mode vamps (unusual for the lane, which is minor-dominant) with a I-V-vi-IV feel and a descending bass\" `[LOW-CONF]`, repeated here — so the keys and scales are major-weighted and the bass carries stepwise descending passing tones. And the fills are \"Italo-dance tom rolls\", which is a tom/tomHigh perc bed with the snare-roll ladder off. The topline is \"thin, high, and chant-adjacent, with an onomatopoeic or nonsense-syllable hook; harmony vocals are close and few\", so the melody sits at 74-96 with a narrow interval spread and the counter is thin at 0.35.",
